findings.issue:
The replacement run is on a different head from the failed run. Only the fixing commit is required to 'remove or weaken no test, gate, or threshold' (line 91). Nothing constrains the other commits between the failed head and the replacing head.

If any of them changes the failed gate, the replacing run can pass for that reason and not because of the fix. Possible changes:
- the gate's entry in engineering/gates.json, such as zig-test timeout_ms 600000;
- the workflow job in .github/workflows/gates.yml (`timeout-minutes`, steps, runner image);
- the locked compiler;
- a test that the failed step runs, such as removing it, skipping it, or adding an exclusion.

This is a live scenario. FP-0098 criterion 4 (plan.json:3445) permits 'a separate reviewed change to the timeout' for exactly the zig-test timeouts this rule targets. A same-head rerun cannot be affected this way, because it reuses the failed head's gate definitions. As written, the text lets a weakening split off into another commit carry an acceptance, against AGENTS.md:61 ('Do not weaken protected gates, exclusions, or performance thresholds').

findings.recommendation:
Require the failure record to:
- name the failed gate and step from the failed run's own receipt or log;
- give evidence that the cause makes that step fail on the failed head's sources, for example FP-0098's measurement of FP-0014 case 46 on the pre-fix tree;
- mark the attribution [INFERENCE] where the failed run kept no output.

If the cause cannot be tied to the failed step even by inference, only a passing same-head rerun is allowed.
